    "https://ufv.njoyn.com/CL3/xweb/xweb.asp?clid=56144&Page=JobDetails&Jobid=J0926-0603&BRID=363808&lang=1": {
      "title": "Officer, Operations & Stewardship",
      "description": "Job postings > Careers | UFV.ca Future Students International Current Students Alumni Academic Calendar myUFV myClass Library UFV / Careers Job postings To display this page you need a browser with JavaScript support. Share Facebook X Email LinkedIn Officer, Operations & Stewardship Relationship and Fund Development Abbotsford (King Road) Application deadline: November 1, 2026 Job Number: J0926-0603 Status: Temporary Open Positions: 1 Salary Schedule: Staff Salary Schedule, Group 7 Hours/Week: 35 Salary: $57,137.65 - $68,268.36/Year Position Description Job Summary The Officer, Operations and Stewardship provides operational support to the Relationship and Fund Development team through the coordination of fundraising administration, donor stewardship, gift processing, advancement systems, communications, and operational activities that support the University's fundraising priorities. The position is responsible for administering gift processing, maintaining the integrity of donor information within Raiser's Edge NXT (RE NXT), coordinating fundraising operations, preparing reports and donor communications, supporting stewardship initiatives, and providing operational and logistical support for fundraising activities and events. Working collaboratively with internal departments and external stakeholders, the incumbent contributes to the efficient delivery of fundraising services and positive donor experiences. As a front-line representative of Relationship and Fund Development, the Officer responds to donor, alumni, and community inquiries, provides professional customer service, and supports donor engagement through responsive communication and effective operational coordination. Qualifications Diploma in Business Administration, Office Administration, Fundraising Management, Public Administration, Philanthropy, or another related discipline from a recognized post-secondary institution. An equivalent combination of education and directly related advancement operations or fundraising administration experience may be considered. A bachelor's degree is considered an asset. Minimum three (3) years of progressively responsible experience in advancement operations, fundraising administration, donor information management, donor stewardship, gift administration, or a comparable advancement, alumni relations, or development environment, preferably within a post-secondary institution or not-for-profit organization. Demonstrated experience using Raiser's Edge NXT or a comparable fundraising CRM/database. Experience supporting gift administration, donor stewardship, financial processes, and data management. Demonstrated intermediate to advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office applications, including Outlook, Word, Excel, Teams, and reporting tools. Experience with fundraising communications, email marketing platforms, event management systems, or online giving platforms is an asset. Experience using project management software such as Wrike an asset. Exceptional verbal and written communication skills, including the ability to communicate clearly, professionally, and diplomatically by telephone, email, and in person with donors, alumni, volunteers, senior university leaders, and community members. Strong analytical, problem-solving, and attention-to-detail skills. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities while maintaining accuracy and confidentiality. Demonstrated experience providing professional customer service in a role requiring frequent verbal communication and relationship management. Demonstrated ability to actively listen, accurately interpret verbal instructions and stakeholder requests, and communicate information clearly and accurately with minimal supervision. Demonstrated ability to prepare, edit, and proofread correspondence while ensuring accuracy, clarity, tone, and consistency. Demonstrated ability to exercise sound judgment when responding to inquiries and representing the University. Excellent interpersonal skills with the ability to build productive relationships across the University and with external partners. Ability to work independently while contributing effectively within a collaborative team environment. Demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, inclusion, accessibility, Indigenization, and reconciliation. How to Apply Please submit your resume and cover letter by the application deadline. About UFV The University of the Fraser Valley is located on the traditional territory of the Halq'eméylem-speaking peoples. We express our gratitude and respect for the honour of living and working in Stó:lo Tém:éxw (Stó:lo Land; Stó:lo World). In all that we do, UFV strives to support and honour the Stó:lo peoples goals of self-determination and well-being on these lands. A commitment to Indigenization and Reconciliation is core to our institutional Vision and Integrated Strategic Plan: IYAQAWTXW — which means House of Transformation. This commitment includes the goal of centering Indigenous ways of knowing throughout our organization, recognizing our responsibilities to community, and a multi-year plan to increase the number of Indigenous faculty, staff, and administrators working at UFV. UFV has campus locations in Abbotsford, Chilliwack, Mission, and Hope in the beautiful British Columbia, and in Chandigarh, India. Recognized as one of BC's top employers, UFV offers a combination of career and lifestyle benefits. Join a team of 1,400 passionate professionals who value integrity, inclusivity and excellence. At UFV, you will serve a vibrant community and shape the future of 15,000 students. Indigenization, equity, and diversity are essential to our work as a university. A diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been historically underrepresented and marginalized. At UFV, we are committed to recruiting a diverse workforce that represents the community we proudly serve. We encourage applications from Indigenous peoples, members of groups that experience discrimination due to race, colour, place of origin, ancestry, and/or religion, persons who identify as women and 2SLGBTQ+, and persons with disabilities. We invite applicants to complete a confidential self-identification survey as part of the application process. All questions are voluntary, with an option to decline to answer. Information will be used to support efforts to broaden the diversity of the recruitment process, and results are not shared with the selection committee. Inquiries about the survey may be directed to careers@ufv.ca. Learn more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. In an effort to be both environmentally and fiscally responsible, UFV will contact only candidates receiving an interview. We thank all applicants for considering UFV for employment. Shortlisted applicants may be required to undergo a criminal record check and/ or a verification of their education credentials. UFV is committed to the principle of equity in employment. Please note: you should receive a confirmation email once you have successfully applied to this position. If you do not, please contact the Njoyn Help Desk at webhelp.njoyn or 1-877-427-7717 Monday to Friday 8am to 8pm (EST). Are you interested in this job? Job Opportunities Career Alert My Profile Help 1-604-854-4554 careers@ufv.ca Future students Academic Calendar Find your program How to Apply Book a tour Transfer credits Tuition & fees FAQs Help Ask a question A to Z websites Course Finder Directory IT Service Desk Reset your password myUFV Search Current Students Advising Centre Bookstore Register for courses Safe Student Community Shuttle bus Student services Timetables myClass About us Accreditation Careers at UFV Cascades Athletics Make a gift Events Human Resources News Contact us Contact Us 1-604-504-7441 1-888-504-7441 Toll-Free Maps Ask a question Copy link ✓ Thanks for sharing! Find any service AddToAny More…"
    },
    "https://ufv.njoyn.com/CL3/xweb/xweb.asp?clid=56144&Page=JobDetails&Jobid=J0626-1023&BRID=363916&lang=1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Planning, Geography, and Environmental Studies",
      "description": "Job postings > Careers | UFV.ca Future Students International Current Students Alumni Academic Calendar myUFV myClass Library UFV / Careers Job postings To display this page you need a browser with JavaScript support. Share Facebook X Email LinkedIn Assistant Professor, Planning, Geography, and Environmental Studies Planning, Geography & Environmental Studies Abbotsford (King Road) Application deadline: January 29, 2027 Job Number: J0626-1023 Status: Permanent Open Positions: 1 Salary Schedule: Teaching Faculty Salary Schedule Hours/Week: 35 Salary: $70,437.00 - $112,872.00/Year Job Summary UFV's Department of Planning, Geography and Environmental Studies (PGES) in the Faculty of Science invites applications for a tenure-track, open-rank Professor of Land-Use Planning with an interest in one or more of the following areas: sustainable development, food security, environmental, and Indigenous community planning. The successful applicant will join a thriving faculty that supports programs in Geography, Environmental Studies, and Geographic Information Systems (GIS). The Bachelor of Regional and Community Planning (BRCP) degree has recently been accredited and has strong enrollment. The successful candidate will help to further grow and strengthen the new planning program in PGES and contribute to practicums and professional experience, PGES programs’ curriculum development and teaching. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate courses and supervise undergraduate research students through studio, applied practicum, and independent study courses. Teaching assignments may include Settlements(PLAN 160), Social Geography: The Urban Experience (GEOG 241), GIS for the Built Environment (GEOG 355), Quantitative Methods in Geography (GEOG 252), Planning Law (PLAN 310) and Indigenous Land Use and Community Planning (PLAN 410), but the final list will be tailored to the expertise of the successful candidate. In addition to teaching, the successful candidate will actively participate in PGES activities, service, events, and initiatives, and will be committed to ongoing professional development. PGES seeks a collegial and energetic individual with exceptional communication skills, demonstrated excellence in teaching at the undergraduate level, and experience in teaching across traditional disciplinary boundaries and delivering courses in community settings. The successful candidate is expected to build relationships with Fraser Valley municipalities and Indigenous communities by providing students with collaborative and integrative applied experiential learning opportunities. Incorporating Indigenous perspectives in the curriculum is essential, as is teaching international students and managing the needs of students in a diverse learning environment. Short-listed applicants will have an emerging record of applied research or professional productivity commensurate with their experience. The ability to attract external funding and establish a successful applied research program at UFV to support research and student learning experiences are highly desirable. The ability to use Geographic Information Systems (GIS), Remote Sensing, statistical software (SPSS/STATA), and emerging technologies such as Artificial Intelligence to support land-use planning will be an asset, as is experience with community outreach in agricultural regions with rapidly growing urban centers. Qualifications Extensive knowledge of land-use planning: urban spatial structure and physical design within urban and regional settings. A master’s degree in urban and regional planning or a closely related field from a recognized post-secondary institution is required, in addition to extensive professional experience. However, a PhD in the mentioned disciplines is preferred. Registered Professional Planner certification with the Canadian Institute of Planners, facilitated by the Planning Standards Board of Canada, is required . Teaching experience at the undergraduate level with recent evidence of teaching excellence is preferred. Familiarity with technology-supported teaching both in the classroom, online and in the planning profession. Effective cross-cultural, interdisciplinary communication skills and experience working with Indigenous communities are assets. A growing list of impactful applied research and/or community-based planning initiatives. Interest in service contribution to PGES, UFV, and Indigenous communities of the Fraser Valley. How to Apply To be considered for this position, please submit a complete application package that includes a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching philosophy statement, research interest statement, samples of scholarly activities (e.g., publication or project portfolio), and three letters of reference. In your application, please indicate your eligibility to work legally in Canada. Applications will be reviewed by the SAC in December 2026, however the posting will be kept open for applications until the end of January 2027. You will receive a confirmation email once you successfully apply for the position. If not, do not hesitate to contact the Njoyn Help Desk at candidate.njoynhelp@cgi.com or 1-877-427-7717, Monday to Friday, 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. (EST). UFV is committed to equity in employment, and all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. UFV will contact only candidates receiving an interview. Shortlisted applicants may be required to undergo a criminal record check and a verification of their education credentials. For further information about the position, please contact Dr. Cherie Enns, RPP, Chair, Bachelor of Regional and Community Planning, University of the Fraser Valley, at cherie.enns@ufv.ca. About UFV The University of the Fraser Valley is located on the traditional territory of the Halq'eméylem-speaking peoples. We express our gratitude and respect for the honour of living and working in Stó:lo Tém:éxw (Stó:lo Land; Stó:lo World). In all that we do, UFV strives to support and honour the Stó:lo peoples goals of self-determination and well-being on these lands. A commitment to Indigenization and Reconciliation is core to our institutional Vision and Integrated Strategic Plan: IYAQAWTXW — which means House of Transformation. This commitment includes the goal of centering Indigenous ways of knowing throughout our organization, recognizing our responsibilities to community, and a multi-year plan to increase the number of Indigenous faculty, staff, and administrators working at UFV. UFV has campus locations in Abbotsford, Chilliwack, Mission, and Hope in the beautiful British Columbia, and in Chandigarh, India. Recognized as one of BC's top employers, UFV offers a combination of career and lifestyle benefits. Join a team of 1,400 passionate professionals who value integrity, inclusivity and excellence. At UFV, you will serve a vibrant community and shape the future of 15,000 students. Indigenization, equity, and diversity are essential to our work as a university. A diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been historically underrepresented and marginalized. At UFV, we are committed to recruiting a diverse workforce that represents the community we proudly serve. We encourage applications from Indigenous peoples, members of groups that experience discrimination due to race, colour, place of origin, ancestry, and/or religion, persons who identify as women and 2SLGBTQ+, and persons with disabilities. We invite applicants to complete a confidential self-identification survey as part of the application process. All questions are voluntary, with an option to decline to answer. Information will be used to support efforts to broaden the diversity of the recruitment process, and results are not shared with the selection committee. Inquiries about the survey may be directed to careers@ufv.ca. Learn more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. In an effort to be both environmentally and fiscally responsible, UFV will contact only candidates receiving an interview. We thank all applicants for considering UFV for employment. Shortlisted applicants may be required to undergo a criminal record check and/ or a verification of their education credentials. UFV is committed to the principle of equity in employment. Please note: you should receive a confirmation email once you have successfully applied to this position. If you do not, please contact the Njoyn Help Desk at webhelp.njoyn or 1-877-427-7717 Monday to Friday 8am to 8pm (EST). Are you interested in this job? Job Opportunities Career Alert My Profile Help 1-604-854-4554 careers@ufv.ca Future students Academic Calendar Find your program How to Apply Book a tour Transfer credits Tuition & fees FAQs Help Ask a question A to Z websites Course Finder Directory IT Service Desk Reset your password myUFV Search Current Students Advising Centre Bookstore Register for courses Safe Student Community Shuttle bus Student services Timetables myClass About us Accreditation Careers at UFV Cascades Athletics Make a gift Events Human Resources News Contact us Contact Us 1-604-504-7441 1-888-504-7441 Toll-Free Maps Ask a question Copy link ✓ Thanks for sharing! Find any service AddToAny More…"
