    "https://ufv.njoyn.com/CL3/xweb/xweb.asp?clid=56144&Page=JobDetails&Jobid=J1026-0159&BRID=364678&lang=1": {
      "title": "Manager, Community Programming and Experience",
      "description": "Job postings > Careers | UFV.ca Future Students International Current Students Alumni Academic Calendar myUFV myClass Library UFV / Careers Job postings To display this page you need a browser with JavaScript support. Share Facebook X Email LinkedIn Manager, Community Programming and Experience Community Programming & Experience Application deadline: October 26, 2026 Job Number: J1026-0159 Status: Permanent Open Positions: 1 Anticipated Start Date: 16/11/2026 Campus(es): Abbotsford (King Road) Hours/Week: 35 % Full Time: 100 Total Months/Year: 12 Salary Schedule: Staff Salary Schedule, Group 10 Salary: $71,898.38 - $84,432.96/Year Position Description Job Summary The Manager, Community Programming and Experience (CPE) provides operational leadership for the day-to-day management of the Community Programming and Experience department, ensuring the effective coordination, delivery, and continuous improvement of community engagement initiatives, signature programs, and institutional events that support the University's community engagement priorities. Reporting to the Director, the Manager oversees departmental operations, supervises staff, monitors program budgets, supports the implementation of approved risk management practices, and ensures compliance with University policies, procedures, and service standards. Through effective operational planning, resource coordination, and people leadership, the Manager supports the successful delivery of more than thirty annual and ongoing initiatives, including Convocation, Open House, National Day for Truth and Reconciliation, International Women's Day, and the Community Report and Celebration. Working closely with the Director, the Strategist, the Coordinator, and internal and external stakeholders, the Manager coordinates operational activities, monitors departmental performance, and ensures staff, resources, and administrative processes are effectively aligned to support the successful delivery of programs and events. The Manager provides operational advice, analysis, and reporting to support departmental planning and informed decision-making; monitors operational risks, budgets, and service standards; and fosters collaborative relationships that contribute to effective service delivery and positive community experiences. Through strong operational leadership and a commitment to continuous improvement, the Manager helps ensure the Community Programming and Experience department delivers high-quality, inclusive, and well-coordinated programs and services that strengthen community engagement and support the University's strategic objectives. Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in a relevant field (e.g., Communications, Public Relations, Project Management, Business Administration, or related discipline) from a recognized, post-secondary institution. Minimum of five (5) years of progressively responsible experience in community engagement, institutional programming, event management, stakeholder relations, and project management. Demonstrated experience leading complex initiatives, supervising staff, managing budgets, and working with diverse internal and external stakeholders. Experience leading teams in a unionized environment. Experience interpreting and applying organizational policies, procedures, and administrative practices. Demonstrated ability to lead change within an operational environment and foster continuous improvement through effective planning, process improvement, and staff engagement. Strong project management skills with the ability to oversee multiple initiatives simultaneously. Excellent communication, relationship-building, and stakeholder engagement skills. Experience monitoring budgets, preparing financial information, and supporting sound financial stewardship. Experience coordinating departmental operations, allocating resources, and monitoring operational performance in a complex organizational environment. Experience mediating diverse stakeholder perspectives and facilitating collaborative decision-making. Demonstrated ability to lead operational planning and translate strategic priorities into actionable work plans. Experience supporting the implementation of organizational risk management practices and operational procedures. Demonstrated experience supervising, coaching, and developing professional staff. Proven ability to develop and manage vendor relationships, contracts, and service agreements. Strong analytical skills with experience evaluating program outcomes and implementing improvements. Demonstrated ability to represent an organization in community-facing roles with professionalism, tact, and diplomacy. Experience coordinating multi-departmental initiatives or working groups. Training or certification in project management (e.g., PMP, CAPM, Agile methodologies) is an asset. How to Apply Please submit your resume and cover letter by the application deadline. About UFV The University of the Fraser Valley is located on the traditional territory of the Halq'eméylem-speaking peoples. We express our gratitude and respect for the honour of living and working in Stó:lo Tém:éxw (Stó:lo Land; Stó:lo World). In all that we do, UFV strives to support and honour the Stó:lo peoples goals of self-determination and well-being on these lands. A commitment to Indigenization and Reconciliation is core to our institutional Vision and Integrated Strategic Plan: IYAQAWTXW — which means House of Transformation. This commitment includes the goal of centering Indigenous ways of knowing throughout our organization, recognizing our responsibilities to community, and a multi-year plan to increase the number of Indigenous faculty, staff, and administrators working at UFV. UFV has campus locations in Abbotsford, Chilliwack, Mission, and Hope in the beautiful British Columbia, and in Chandigarh, India. Recognized as one of BC's top employers, UFV offers a combination of career and lifestyle benefits. Join a team of 1,400 passionate professionals who value integrity, inclusivity and excellence. At UFV, you will serve a vibrant community and shape the future of 15,000 students. Indigenization, equity, and diversity are essential to our work as a university. A diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been historically underrepresented and marginalized. At UFV, we are committed to recruiting a diverse workforce that represents the community we proudly serve. We encourage applications from Indigenous peoples, members of groups that experience discrimination due to race, colour, place of origin, ancestry, and/or religion, persons who identify as women and 2SLGBTQ+, and persons with disabilities. We invite applicants to complete a confidential self-identification survey as part of the application process. All questions are voluntary, with an option to decline to answer. Information will be used to support efforts to broaden the diversity of the recruitment process, and results are not shared with the selection committee. Inquiries about the survey may be directed to careers@ufv.ca. Learn more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. In an effort to be both environmentally and fiscally responsible, UFV will contact only candidates receiving an interview. We thank all applicants for considering UFV for employment. Shortlisted applicants may be required to undergo a criminal record check and/ or a verification of their education credentials. UFV is committed to the principle of equity in employment. Please note: you should receive a confirmation email once you have successfully applied to this position. If you do not, please contact the Njoyn Help Desk at webhelp.njoyn or 1-877-427-7717 Monday to Friday 8am to 8pm (EST). Are you interested in this job? Job Opportunities Career Alert My Profile Help 1-604-854-4554 careers@ufv.ca Future students Academic Calendar Find your program How to Apply Book a tour Transfer credits Tuition & fees FAQs Help Ask a question A to Z websites Course Finder Directory IT Service Desk Reset your password myUFV Search Current Students Advising Centre Bookstore Register for courses Safe Student Community Shuttle bus Student services Timetables myClass About us Accreditation Careers at UFV Cascades Athletics Make a gift Events Human Resources News Contact us Contact Us 1-604-504-7441 1-888-504-7441 Toll-Free Maps Ask a question Copy link ✓ Thanks for sharing! Find any service AddToAny More…"
    },
    "https://ufv.njoyn.com/CL3/xweb/xweb.asp?clid=56144&Page=JobDetails&Jobid=J1026-0323&BRID=364675&lang=1": {
      "title": "Manager, Procurement",
      "description": "Job postings > Careers | UFV.ca Future Students International Current Students Alumni Academic Calendar myUFV myClass Library UFV / Careers Job postings To display this page you need a browser with JavaScript support. Share Facebook X Email LinkedIn Manager, Procurement Financial Services Application deadline: October 26, 2026 Job Number: J1026-0323 Status: Permanent Open Positions: 1 Campus(es): Abbotsford (Clearbrook) Hours/Week: 35 % Full Time: 100 Total Months/Year: 12 Salary Schedule: Exempt Salary Schedule, Group 11 Salary: $94,443.00 - $138,516.00/Year Compensation Details: Compensation beyond the mid-point requires approval by the Public Sector Employers' Council Secretariat (PSEC). Position Description Job Summary The Manager, Procurement, leads and oversees the university's Procurement function, ensuring the effective, compliant, and value-driven acquisition of goods and services in support of institutional objectives. Reporting to the Director, Financial Services, the role provides leadership to the Procurement team and serves as a trusted advisor to the university community on procurement, sourcing, contracting, and supplier management matters. The Manager promotes best practices, continuous improvement, operational efficiency, and risk management while fostering strong relationships with internal stakeholders, suppliers, and external partners. This role oversees the procurement process from planning and competitive solicitation through contract administration and supplier management, ensuring the acquisition of goods and services is conducted in a fair, transparent, cost-effective, and compliant manner consistent with university policy, applicable legislation, and public sector procurement standards. Qualifications Bachelor's Degree in Business Administration, Commerce, Supply Chain Management, Public Administration, or related discipline. Professional designation in supply chain or procurement management, such as SCMP (Supply Chain Management Professional), CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management), or equivalent designation. Minimum five (5) years of progressively responsible procurement, sourcing, and contract management experience, preferably within a public sector, broader public sector, or post-secondary environment. Demonstrated experience leading competitive procurement processes, including Requests for Proposals (RFPs), Requests for Quotations (RFQs), Invitations to Tender (ITTs), and other public procurement methodologies. Experience developing, negotiating, administering, and managing contracts and supplier agreements. Experience interpreting and applying procurement policies, trade agreements, applicable legislation, and public procurement best practices. Demonstrated ability to provide procurement guidance and support to a diverse range of internal and external stakeholders. Proven leadership experience, including managing staff and supporting the development, implementation, and continuous improvement of procurement systems, processes, and operational effectiveness. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple projects, priorities, and competing deadlines in a complex organizational environment. Experience identifying and implementing process improvements, cost-saving opportunities, and operational efficiencies while maintaining service quality and value. Strong analytical, problem-solving, negotiation, communication, and relationship management skills. How to Apply Please submit your resume and cover letter by the application deadline. About UFV The University of the Fraser Valley is located on the traditional territory of the Halq'eméylem-speaking peoples. We express our gratitude and respect for the honour of living and working in Stó:lo Tém:éxw (Stó:lo Land; Stó:lo World). In all that we do, UFV strives to support and honour the Stó:lo peoples goals of self-determination and well-being on these lands. A commitment to Indigenization and Reconciliation is core to our institutional Vision and Integrated Strategic Plan: IYAQAWTXW — which means House of Transformation. This commitment includes the goal of centering Indigenous ways of knowing throughout our organization, recognizing our responsibilities to community, and a multi-year plan to increase the number of Indigenous faculty, staff, and administrators working at UFV. UFV has campus locations in Abbotsford, Chilliwack, Mission, and Hope in the beautiful British Columbia, and in Chandigarh, India. Recognized as one of BC's top employers, UFV offers a combination of career and lifestyle benefits. Join a team of 1,400 passionate professionals who value integrity, inclusivity and excellence. At UFV, you will serve a vibrant community and shape the future of 15,000 students. Indigenization, equity, and diversity are essential to our work as a university. A diverse community fosters the inclusion of voices that have been historically underrepresented and marginalized. At UFV, we are committed to recruiting a diverse workforce that represents the community we proudly serve. We encourage applications from Indigenous peoples, members of groups that experience discrimination due to race, colour, place of origin, ancestry, and/or religion, persons who identify as women and 2SLGBTQ+, and persons with disabilities. We invite applicants to complete a confidential self-identification survey as part of the application process. All questions are voluntary, with an option to decline to answer. Information will be used to support efforts to broaden the diversity of the recruitment process, and results are not shared with the selection committee. Inquiries about the survey may be directed to careers@ufv.ca. Learn more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. In an effort to be both environmentally and fiscally responsible, UFV will contact only candidates receiving an interview. We thank all applicants for considering UFV for employment. Shortlisted applicants may be required to undergo a criminal record check and/ or a verification of their education credentials. UFV is committed to the principle of equity in employment. Please note: you should receive a confirmation email once you have successfully applied to this position. If you do not, please contact the Njoyn Help Desk at webhelp.njoyn or 1-877-427-7717 Monday to Friday 8am to 8pm (EST). Are you interested in this job? Job Opportunities Career Alert My Profile Help 1-604-854-4554 careers@ufv.ca Future students Academic Calendar Find your program How to Apply Book a tour Transfer credits Tuition & fees FAQs Help Ask a question A to Z websites Course Finder Directory IT Service Desk Reset your password myUFV Search Current Students Advising Centre Bookstore Register for courses Safe Student Community Shuttle bus Student services Timetables myClass About us Accreditation Careers at UFV Cascades Athletics Make a gift Events Human Resources News Contact us Contact Us 1-604-504-7441 1-888-504-7441 Toll-Free Maps Ask a question Copy link ✓ Thanks for sharing! Find any service AddToAny More…"
